      <div class='static-page__title-row'>
        <h1 class='static-page__title--sinai'>Sinai Library Digitization Project</h1>
      </div>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          St. Catherine’s Monastery of the Sinai, the Early Manuscripts Electronic Library (EMEL), and the UCLA Library have partnered to digitize the Monastery’s unparalleled manuscript collection and build an online, searchable digital library that makes the results accessible to scholars and the public.  Phase One (February 2018 through March 2022) focused on the Monastery’s 1,172 Arabic and Syriac manuscripts.  Phase Two will turn to its collections in Greek, Georgian, Slavonic, and other languages — 3,387 manuscripts in total.
        </p>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          The resulting digital library allows users to search metadata gathered from a variety of sources, identify manuscripts of interest, and zoom in, pan across, and compare images of different manuscripts.  Beyond the basic metadata drawn from handlists and catalogues, scholarly description of the manuscripts is a separate undertaking from the digitization project itself.  Description projects completed to date include the Syriac Parchment Descriptions Project, which provides comprehensive text identification and description for the Syriac parchment manuscripts in the collection.
        </p>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          By these means, the Sinai Manuscripts Digital Library preserves the contents of the Monastery’s unique manuscript library and enables scholars and interested persons around the world to study outstanding images of the manuscripts and add to our knowledge of the Monastery’s library and its history.
        </p>
      </div>

      <hr class='divider'>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <h3 class='static-page__subtitle static-page__subtitle--sinai'>Funding and Sponsorship</h3>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
        The project is grateful for the support of His Eminence Archbishop Damianos of Sinai, Faran and Raitho; the Holy Council of St. Catherine’s Monastery; and of the Egyptian State and its Ministry of Tourism & Antiquities. The project has received generous financial support from the Ahmanson Foundation, the Arcadia Fund, the National Endowment for the Humanities, the Museum of the Bible Scholars Initiative, and the Steinmetz Foundation.
        </p>
      </div>

        <div class='static-page__row'>
        <h3 class='static-page__subtitle static-page__subtitle--sinai'>Digitization Standards and Methods</h3>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          The project has installed four digitization workstations in a large room graciously provided by the Monastery and recently renovated by the Saint Catherine Foundation.  Digitization personnel undertake six four-week imaging sessions annually.  Captured images range from 600 to 1,100 ppi depending on the size of manuscript and meet or exceed relevant standards.
          </p>
          <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
            The first consideration when digitizing medieval manuscripts, especially in the low-humidity conditions of the Sinai, is the safe handling of the manuscripts.  To accommodate manuscripts in different states of preservation, the project is operating two different materials handling solutions.
          </p>
          <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
            <i>The Preservation Book Cradle (PBC)</i> by Stokes Imaging (Austin, TX) is designed to support fragile parchment codices.  It uses computer-controlled motors to rotate the spine of a fragile codex, following the natural movement of the codex as its pages are turned.
          </p>
          <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
            <i>Modified BC100</i> by Digital Transitions (New York) supports the efficient digitization of stable codices by using two cameras to capture both pages of an open book simultaneously.  To make the BC100 appropriate for medieval manuscripts, EMEL introduced a customized frame that restrains fragile folios with flexible tabs.
          </p>
          <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          <div class="grid__img-wrapper--sinai-about2"></div>
          <div class="grid__img-wrapper--sinai-caption">
            <i>Digitization room at St. Catherine’s Monastery with four imaging stations: two Preservation Book Cradles by Stokes Imaging on the left and in the center; and two dual-camera BC100s by Digital Transitions on the far right (Photo provided by Damianos Kasotakis)</i>
          </div>
          </p>
      </div>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <h3 class='static-page__subtitle static-page__subtitle--sinai'>Consulting Conservators</h3>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
        Consulting conservators review and monitor the protocols and procedures that guide the selection, handling and tracking of manuscripts during the digitization process.  They compared the environmental conditions between the library and the digitization room to ensure that manuscripts can be safely transferred between the two locations.  Consulting conservators also work with the Monastery Librarian to evaluate fragile codices prior to digitization.
        </p>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          <div class="grid__img-wrapper--sinai-about1"></div>
          <div class="grid__img-wrapper--sinai-caption">
            <i>Camera operator using a dual-camera BC100, with modifications by EMEL, at St. Catherine's Monastery (Photo provided by Father Justin Sinaites)</i>
          </div>
        </p>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          <div class="grid__img-wrapper--sinai-about3"></div>
          <div class="grid__img-wrapper--sinai-caption">
            <i>Camera operator using a Preservation Book Cradle by Stokes Imaging, designed for fragile codices, at St. Catherine's Monastery (Photo provided by Damianos Kasotakis)</i>
          </div>
        </p>
      </div>

